{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And they shall no more sacrifice their sacrifices to the goat-demons.</b> The Israelites in Egypt were drawn after this wisdom and followed it greatly, to know future events. They are called שעירים because they appear in the likeness of goats, or on account of that a man shudders (משתער) at seeing them. And likewise they are called שדים because they dwell in waste (שדוד) and desolate places, such as the wildernesses and the desolate extremities of the north where there is no settlement at all because of the extreme cold." "<b>And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote</b> that the core of their existence is from two light elements, and they are fire and air, and therefore they fly because of their subtlety and lightness. And just as all created beings in this lower world in general — whether living creatures, plants, or the mineral — are composed of four elements, so too the creation of the shedim in the work of Genesis was composed of these two elements. But those composed of four elements are a coarse body, perceptible to human apprehension, whereas the shedim composed of these two elements are a subtle body, not perceptible and not apprehended by human senses; rather, they take on form and clothe themselves in shape before the eyes of beholders. They are thus a spiritual body, for these elements are spiritual. And just as for those composed of four elements, their coming-into-being and continuance is the joining of the elements, and their health is the tempering of the elements, and they sicken when one overpowers the other, and their death is their separation one from another — so too the coming-into-being and continuance of the shedim is the joining of the two elements, and their death is in their separation one from another. And this is what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: six things were stated concerning shedim — three like the ministering angels: they have wings like the ministering angels, and they fly like the ministering angels, and they know what is destined to be like the ministering angels — 'they know,' you might say? Rather, say: they hear what is destined to be, like the ministering angels." "<b>And the explanation of the matter is</b> that because they dwell in the air, in the spheres of the elements, they understand future events from the princes of the constellations. And this is an a fortiori argument from birds: if birds know future events from them and declare them in their chirping, and from the birds the diviners skilled and expert in that wisdom know them — then a fortiori the shedim, for whom it is possible to know this from the princes of the constellations. And what the shedim know — and the birds as well — is only the near future, not one far off by many days. Three like humans: they eat and drink like humans, they reproduce like humans, and they die like humans. The intent here is not human food, for a human, who is composed of four elements and is a coarse, perceptible body — his food is like himself, thick and coarse. But the shedim, who are a subtle, spiritual body — their food too is like themselves, subtle, and their eating and drinking is the vapor of fire and the moisture of water: for the portion of fire within them dries out the portion of air within them, and it needs to restore the moisture that was lost from it. This is 'they eat and drink like humans,' for a human's food is on this very pattern: for the moisture of his body dries up within him for two reasons — one, because of the air surrounding him from without, and the second, because of the natural heat that dries his moisture from within; therefore he needs food to restore the moisture lost from him. And such is the food of the shedim, for they lick the moisture of the waters, in the manner of what is written (I Kings 18:35): ״ואת המים אשר בתעלה לחכה״. And this is the meaning of the incense-offering that is offered to them, for the rising smoke and that moisture is their food; they benefit from this and attach themselves to a person and tell him future events, as is known in that wisdom called nigromantia. And the meaning of the death in them that they mentioned — 'they die like humans' — is just as a human's death is the separation of the joining of his four elements, so the death of the shedim is the separation of the joining of their elements. And the meaning of the flying that they mentioned — 'they fly like the ministering angels' — is because of the great lightness of their two elements; and this is an a fortiori argument from birds: if a body composed of four elements flies in the air with great lightness because fire and air predominate in it and water and earth are very slight in it — then all the more so the shedim, who are of the elements of fire and air alone, which are light elements, and of the heavy elements they have nothing at all."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:26:19.554652082Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}